  "raw": {
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/springfieldcollege/jobs/5484218/adjunct-for-the-department-of-psychology": {
      "title": "Adjunct for the Department of Psychology",
      "description": "Adjunct for the Department of Psychology Salary See Position Description Location Springfield, MA Job Type Adjunct Job Number 00443 Department Psychology Opening Date 09/16/2026 Description Benefits Description Springfield College invites applications for adjuncts with expertise in Psychology to support the Psychology Department. The Psychology Department, housed in the School of Arts and Sciences, is a vibrant department that includes a large undergraduate major, a master's level program in Athletic Counseling, and a Counseling Psychology, Psy.D. program. We are seeking instructors to teach a variety of undergraduate Psychology courses, including, but not limited to: Health Psychology, Abnormal Psychology, Motivation and Learning, Research Methods, and Social Psychology. Compensation for adjunct faculty with fewer than 3 years college teaching experience is $2820 for a 3-credit course. Compensation for adjunct faculty with 3 or more years college teaching experience is $3513 for a 3-credit course. Responsibilities The Psychology Department is looking for individuals to help support the instruction of courses in the Undergraduate program, Athletic Counseling master’s program, and Counseling Psychology Doctoral program. Specific courses include: Fieldwork supervision (applicant must be an approved CMPC supervisor), Social Psychology, Research Methods, History and Systems and/or Psychology of Diversity. Qualifications At minimum, applicants must have master’s degree in a Psychology-related field to quality for the position. Must reside in one of the following states: MA, CT, WI, DE, TX, VT, FL Review of applications will begin immediately and will continue until the position is filled. Please submit a letter of interest that details your qualifications to teach in Psychology, your resume/CV, and the names and contact information for 3 professional references. This position is not eligible for benefits. Employer Springfield College Address 263 Alden Street Springfield, Massachusetts, 01109 Phone 413-748-5678 Website http://www.springfield.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/springfieldcollege/jobs/5368082/adjunct-instructor-for-the-department-of-occupational-therapy": {
      "title": "Adjunct Instructor for the Department of Occupational Therapy",
      "description": "Adjunct Instructor for the Department of Occupational Therapy Salary See Position Description Location Springfield, MA Job Type Adjunct Job Number 00396 Department Occupational Therapy Opening Date 06/05/2026 Description Benefits Description Springfield College invites applications for part-time adjunct faculty positions in the Department of Occupational Therapy. The Department is seeking individuals who are passionate about the profession and support our goal to prepare professionals for leadership in the use of human occupation to promote people’s participation in meaningful life roles and activities. Adjunct positions may teach in our undergraduate, MSOT and/or OTD programs. Available opportunities may include Neurology, Adult/older adult rehabilitation, Management/Entrepreneurship, Research/Evidence Based Practice, and Fieldwork preparation seminars. Compensation for adjunct faculty with fewer than 3 years college teaching experience is $2820 for a 3-credit course Compensation for adjunct faculty with 3 or more years college teaching experience is $3513 for a 3-credit course. Responsibilities - Teach and facilitate lecture and/or lab courses within the Department of Occupational Therapy at our Springfield College Campus. - Provide timely and constructive feedback to students on assignments and discussions. - Facilitate active communication with students to support their learning and professional growth. - Collaborate with the OT Program Director and other faculty to ensure course content meets program standards and objectives. - Participate in curriculum development and course revision as needed. Qualifications Required Qualifications: - Initial certification as an occupational therapist - OT Licensure or eligible for OT licensure in Massachusetts - Strong commitment to student success and academic excellence - Excellent communication and interpersonal skills. - Ability to work independently and as part of a team - Documented experience in content area of course focus - Must reside in one of the following states: MA, CT, WI, DE, TX, VT, FL Preferred Qualifications: - At Least 1 year of clinical experience - Previous teaching experience and/or serving as a fieldwork educator - Familiarity with online learning systems such as Brightspace Application Process: Applicants should submit the following materials: - Cover letter detailing their interest in the position, potential course content/topics, and relevant experience including prior online teaching experience and scholarship development. - Curriculum Vitae (CV) highlighting academic and professional achievements. Application Deadline: Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis until the positions are filled. This position is not eligible for benefits. Employer Springfield College Address 263 Alden Street Springfield, Massachusetts, 01109 Phone 413-748-5678 Website http://www.springfield.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/springfieldcollege/jobs/5484183/assistant-professor-in-athletic-training-and-director-of-athletic-healthcare": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Athletic Training and Director of Athletic Healthcare",
      "description": "Assistant Professor in Athletic Training and Director of Athletic Healthcare Salary $90,000.00 - $96,000.00 Annually Location Springfield, MA Job Type Fiscal Faculty - 12 months Job Number 00444 Department Athletic Training Opening Date 09/16/2026 Description Benefits Description Springfield College seeks applications for a Director of Athletic Healthcare position. This individual will serve as a faculty member in the Department of Athletic Training with responsibility for teaching coursework in fall, spring and summer. The Director will be responsible for providing comprehensive athletic healthcare and administrative oversite of the athletic healthcare team while simultaneously providing education, supervision and instruction to athletic training students. This is a full-time, 12-month, program-track appointment within the School of Physical Education, Performance and Sport Leadership beginning January 2027. Expertise and leadership in the field of athletic training, demonstrated excellence in college-level and clinical teaching, and a strong commitment to service and professional activities is expected. Actual salaries will vary depending on multiple factors, including but not limited to degrees attained, experience, and other considerations permitted by law. Comprehensive compensation details, including any additional benefits, will be communicated upon finalization of the employment offer. Responsibilities The Director is responsible for: • Strategic and operational leadership of athletic healthcare services, including maintenance of a wholistic integrated health and wellness athletic healthcare team consisting of graduate fellows, Resident athletic trainers and athletic training faculty members. • Teaching courses within the CAATE accredited MSAT program • Maintaining a budget , managing inventory and completing purchasing. • Exercising independent medical authority for health care decisions related to the diagnosis, treatment, rehabilitation, and return to play of student athletes while performing professional duties of athletic training related to specific sport-specific responsibilities, including attendance at team practices and competitions. • Organizing and conducting medical clearance and pre participation physicals for student athletes. • Maintaining electronic medical records for all student athletes. • Working collaboratively with the Department of Athletics' Leadership Team. • Serving as primary liaison for Athletic Healthcare services with the Team Physician, athletics insurance provider, the Director of the Counseling Center, the Director of the Health Center and the Director of the Strength and Conditioning. • Continuing NCAA and NEWMAC health and safety compliance • Adhering to all NATA standards and guidelines regarding care of student athletes • Development, implementation, routine assessment and updating of athletic healthcare policies and procedures. • Serving as a Clinical Preceptor for the CAATE accredited MSAT program providing clinical education, supervision, and instruction to athletic training students. Qualifications Required Qualifications: The successful candidate must possess exemplary organizational and communication skills and have at least two years of college teaching and/or head/director-level athletic trainer experience who can bring diverse perspectives to this growing department and who are motivated to help students succeed. 4 or more years of experience as a certified athletic trainer, licensed or eligible for licensure in Massachusetts and an NPI number. Candidates must possess strong clinical, administrative, and technology skills. Interested candidates for this position will have an earned degree in Athletic Training from a CAATE-accredited institution, preference will be giving to individuals who have a post-professional doctoral degree from a regionally accredited institution in Athletic Training or a related discipline. Candidates must have demonstrated contemporary expertise in one or more of the following areas: urgent and emergent care, primary care, therapeutic intervention or orthopedics. The environment of the athletic training clinic fosters collaboration between faculty, staff, graduate teaching fellows, residents and athletic training students. Excellent interpersonal and communication skills are essential as well as the ability to work cooperatively with the sports medicine team. Preferred Qualifications: Preferred qualifications include 5 or more years of experience with administrative responsibilities, 3 or more years’ experience as a preceptor, and 3 or more years’ experience teaching college-level courses, and CPR/AED instructor certification. Experience using The Athletic Trainer System also preferred. At Springfield College we offer competitive and generous benefit options for employees and their families. Tuition benefits for employees and dependents Tuition exchange program for dependents Generous paid time off benefits Paid Sick Leave Benefits 403(b) retirement plan (TIAA) with a generous college match Medical, Dental and Vision plan(s) Health Reimbursement Arrangement (HRA) Flexible Spending Accounts (health care expenses and dependent care) Life and accident insurance plans Long-term disability Optional voluntary benefits Employee Assistance Program (EAP) Wellness benefits and programs Free use of our state-of-the-art Wellness Center Part-time employees earn pro-rated holiday, vacation and sick leave. To learn more about our Benefit Package, visit our Human Resources page. Employer Springfield College Address 263 Alden Street Springfield, Massachusetts, 01109 Phone 413-748-5678 Website http://www.springfield.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/springfieldcollege/jobs/5460130/assistant-associate-professor-of-social-work-and-master-of-social-work-program-di": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor of Social Work and Master of Social Work Program Director",
      "description": "Assistant/Associate Professor of Social Work and Master of Social Work Program Director Salary $94,000.00 - $99,500.00 Annually Location Springfield, MA Job Type Fiscal Faculty - 12 months Job Number 00437 Department Social Work Opening Date 08/25/2026 Description Benefits Description The Springfield College Department of Social Work invites applications for a full-time, twelve-month, tenure-track Assistant or Associate Professor of Social Work who will also serve as the MSW Program Director to start July 1, 2027, or January 1, 2027 for exceptional candidates. The Department of Social Work at Springfield College fosters a collaborative and inclusive environment, preparing students to be leaders and advocates for social change. Located in Springfield, MA, we offer opportunities to engage with diverse communities and address pressing social issues through teaching, research, and service. Housed in the School of Health Sciences, the Department of Social Work offers BSW and MSW degrees, accredited by CSWE. Within the MSW program, students can complete a 100% online program, or an in-person weekday or evening program. In addition to social work, the School of Health Sciences includes programs in physician assistant studies, physical therapy, communication sciences and disorders, public health, occupational therapy, and health science. The school is housed in Blake Hall and in the spectacular new Brown Cooper Health Sciences Center, with stellar facilities, including an interprofessional simulation suite featuring high-fidelity simulation and standardized participant programming; a maker space enabling a vast array of creative projects supporting health and wellness; an activities of daily living suite to promote real-life applications of rehabilitation practices; and technology-supported classrooms and conference rooms. Interdisciplinary collaboration, professionalism, engaged student learning, as well as global, intergenerational, and multicultural perspectives are priorities of the School. Founded in 1885, Springfield College is a private, coeducational institution offering undergraduate and graduate programs that reflect its distinctive Humanics Philosophy – the education of the whole person in spirit, mind, and body for leadership in service to humanity. Springfield College offers over 50 undergraduate and 14 graduate areas of study. It is world renowned as the birthplace of basketball. Springfield College is consistently ranked in the top tier in its category – Master’s-North Region - in the “America’s Best Colleges” issue of “U.S. News & World Report. For more information, please visit the College’s homepage at www.springfieldcollege.edu. Springfield College welcomes candidates from all backgrounds and lived experiences, who will contribute to the campus community. Actual salaries will vary depending on multiple factors, including but not limited to degrees attained, experience, and other considerations permitted by law. Comprehensive compensation details, including any additional benefits, will be communicated upon finalization of the employment offer. Responsibilities - Serve as the MSW Program Director, providing leadership and oversight of the program, including curriculum development, CSWE accreditation, student recruitment, and faculty mentoring. - Work closely with the Practicum Director and Practicum Support Specialist to ensure strong practicum education - Teach graduate-level social work courses with a focus on policy, research, and related areas, both in-person and online. - The college subscribes to the Boyer areas of scholarship. Candidates should have an active research agenda (or promise of) in any of the Boyer areas of scholarship that has potential for dissemination beyond the college. - Mentor and advise MSW students, supporting their academic and professional development. - Foster an inclusive learning environment that reflects the diverse backgrounds and experiences of our student population. - Collaborate with department faculty and university administration to ensure the success of the MSW program. Qualifications Required Qualifications Applicants must have a Master of Social Work (MSW) degree from a CSWE-accredited program, and be licensed or eligible for licensure in social work. Evidence of experience in academic leadership or program administration and curriculum development and CSWE accreditation processes is required. Preferred Qualifications Successful candidates will have a record of teaching excellence, including experience with online and hybrid learning modalities and an earned doctoral degree in social work or a closely related field by position start date. Evidence of experience in academic leadership or program administration and curriculum development and CSWE accreditation processes will strengthen the application. The ability to work collaboratively with colleagues, community partners, and other stakeholders with strong communication, collaboration, and organizational skills to work effectively with students, faculty, practicum educators, and community partners should be evident in applicant materials. A track record of securing external funding for research or program development will further strengthen the application. Applicants must submit a letter of intent, current curriculum vitae, and the names, addresses, phone numbers, and e-mail addresses of three professional references. Application reviews are ongoing and will continue until the position is filled. At Springfield College we offer competitive and generous benefit options for employees and their families. Tuition benefits for employees and dependents Tuition exchange program for dependents Generous paid time off benefits Paid Sick Leave Benefits 403(b) retirement plan (TIAA) with a generous college match Medical, Dental and Vision plan(s) Health Reimbursement Arrangement (HRA) Flexible Spending Accounts (health care expenses and dependent care) Life and accident insurance plans Long-term disability Optional voluntary benefits Employee Assistance Program (EAP) Wellness benefits and programs Free use of our state-of-the-art Wellness Center Part-time employees earn pro-rated holiday, vacation and sick leave. To learn more about our Benefit Package, visit our Human Resources page. Employer Springfield College Address 263 Alden Street Springfield, Massachusetts, 01109 Phone 413-748-5678 Website http://www.springfield.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/springfieldcollege/jobs/5365816/assistant-associate-professor-or-professor-of-physical-therapy": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor or Professor of Physical Therapy",
      "description": "Assistant/Associate Professor or Professor of Physical Therapy Salary $78,000.00 - $105,000.00 Annually Location Springfield, MA Job Type Academic Faculty - 9 months Job Number 00392 Department Physical Therapy Opening Date 06/04/2026 Description Benefits Description The Springfield College Department of Physical Therapy invites applications for a full-time, nine-month, tenure-track faculty position at the assistant professor, associate professor or professor level, commensurate with experience, beginning Fall 2026. Housed in the School of Health Sciences, the Department of Physical Therapy offers a 6.5-year dual-degree program leading to both a bachelor’s degree in health science/pre-physical therapy and a Doctor of Physical Therapy degree. Using a collaborative and cooperative model, we are a faculty of academicians and clinicians who foster the acquisition of knowledge, values, and skills for exemplary professional practice. We develop innovative leadership and academic programs so that students, faculty, and consumers of physical therapy services may reach their fullest potential. Our service and scholarship reflect a commitment to the enrichment and promotion of the physical therapy profession and to our greater community. In addition to physical therapy, the School of Health Sciences includes programs in physician assistant studies, occupational therapy, communication sciences and disorders, public health, social work, and health science. The school is housed in the spectacular new Brown Cooper Health Sciences Center, with stellar facilities, including an interprofessional simulation suite featuring high-fidelity simulation and standardized participant programming; a maker space enabling a vast array of creative projects supporting health and wellness; an activities of daily living suite to promote real-life applications of rehabilitation practices; a spacious sunlit human anatomy lab; and technology-supported classrooms and conference rooms. Interdisciplinary collaboration, professionalism, engaged student learning, as well as global, intergenerational, and multicultural perspectives are priorities of the School. Founded in 1885, Springfield College is a private, coeducational institution offering undergraduate and graduate programs that reflect its distinctive Humanics Philosophy – the education of the whole person in spirit, mind, and body for leadership in service to humanity. Springfield College offers over 50 undergraduate and 14 graduate areas of study. It is world renowned as the birthplace of basketball. Springfield College is consistently ranked in the top tier in its category – Master’s-North Region - in the “America’s Best Colleges” issue of “U.S. News & World Report. For more information, please visit the College’s homepage at www.springfieldcollege.edu . Actual salaries will vary depending on multiple factors, including but not limited to degrees attained, experience, and other considerations permitted by law. Comprehensive compensation details, including any additional benefits, will be communicated upon finalization of the employment offer. Responsibilities Primary responsibilities include teaching clinical and foundational science course work in our Doctor of Physical Therapy program and other graduate health science programs. Additional responsibilities include scholarly activity, student advisement and mentorship, and service to the Department, School, and College. Qualifications - Candidates are required to hold an earned academic doctorate (e.g., PhD, EdD, DSc, DHsc, or equivalent) by the official start date of employment in a relevant field including but not limited to physical therapy, occupational therapy, exercise physiology, anatomy, kinesiology, or biomechanics. - Successful candidates will demonstrate prior teaching experience in physical therapy, a minimum of five years of relevant professional experience, and a record of scholarship. - Possession of an American Board of Physical Therapy Specialty (ABPTS) ispreferred, but not required. Applicants must submit a letter of intent, current curriculum vitae, and the names, addresses, phone numbers, and e-mail addresses of three professional references. Application reviews are ongoing and will continue until the position is filled. At Springfield College we offer competitive and generous benefit options for employees and their families. Tuition benefits for employees and dependents Tuition exchange program for dependents Generous paid time off benefits Paid Sick Leave Benefits 403(b) retirement plan (TIAA) with a generous college match Medical, Dental and Vision plan(s) Health Reimbursement Arrangement (HRA) Flexible Spending Accounts (health care expenses and dependent care) Life and accident insurance plans Long-term disability Optional voluntary benefits Employee Assistance Program (EAP) Wellness benefits and programs Free use of our state-of-the-art Wellness Center Part-time employees earn pro-rated holiday, vacation and sick leave. To learn more about our Benefit Package, visit our Human Resources page. Employer Springfield College Address 263 Alden Street Springfield, Massachusetts, 01109 Phone 413-748-5678 Website http://www.springfield.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/springfieldcollege/jobs/5219930/associate-professor-or-professor-of-physical-therapy-and-dpt-program-director": {
      "title": "Associate Professor or Professor of Physical Therapy and DPT Program Director",
      "description": "Associate Professor or Professor of Physical Therapy and DPT Program Director Salary $120,000.00 - $148,000.00 Annually Location Springfield, MA Job Type Fiscal Faculty - 12 months Job Number 00332 Department Physical Therapy Opening Date 06/15/2026 Description Benefits Description The Springfield College Department of Physical Therapy invites applications for a full-time twelve-month tenure-track position at the associate professor or professor level for the position of Department Chair and Doctor of Physical Therapy Program Director, beginning in July 2026. The Department of Physical Therapy offers a 6.5-year dual-degree program leading to both a bachelor’s degree in health science/pre-physical therapy and a Doctor of Physical Therapy degree. Using a collaborative and cooperative model, we are a faculty of academicians and clinicians who foster the acquisition of knowledge, values, and skills for exemplary professional practice. We develop innovative leadership and academic programs so that students, faculty, and consumers of physical therapy services may reach their fullest potential. Our service and scholarship reflect a commitment to the enrichment and promotion of the physical therapy profession and to our greater community. In addition to Physical Therapy, the School of Health Sciences includes programs in Communication Sciences and Disorders, Health Science, Occupational Therapy, Physician Assistant Studies, Public Health, and Social Work. The school is housed in the spectacular new Brown Cooper Health Sciences Center, with stellar facilities, including an interprofessional simulation suite featuring all-new high-fidelity simulation and standardized participant programming; a maker space enabling a vast array of creative projects supporting health and wellness; an activities of daily living suite to promote real-life applications of rehabilitation practices; a spacious sunlit human anatomy lab; and technology-supported classrooms and conference rooms. Interdisciplinary collaboration, professionalism, engaged student learning, as well as global, intergenerational, and multicultural perspectives are priorities of the School. Founded in 1885, Springfield College is a private, coeducational institution offering undergraduate and graduate programs that reflect its distinctive Humanics Philosophy – the education of the whole person in spirit, mind, and body for leadership in service to humanity. Springfield College offers over 50 undergraduate and 14 graduate areas of study. It is world renowned as the birthplace of basketball. Springfield College is consistently ranked in the top tier in its category – Master’s-North Region - in the “America’s Best Colleges” issue of “U.S. News & World Report. For more information, please visit the College’s homepage at www.springfieldcollege.edu . Actual salaries will vary depending on multiple factors, including but not limited to degrees attained, experience, and other considerations permitted by law. Comprehensive compensation details, including any additional benefits, will be communicated upon finalization of the employment offer. Responsibilities Primary responsibilities for this position are serving as DPT program director and department chair. At Springfield College, the role of department chair also includes additional stipends and administrative workload. The position includes teaching and advising as well as participating in scholarly endeavors and providing service within the College and community. Qualifications Required Qualifications: - Rank of associate professor or professor - Earned academic doctoral degree (e.g., PhD, EdD) in physical therapy or a related discipline - Licensure or licensure-eligible in physical therapy in Massachusetts - Minimum of 6 years of full-time equivalent experience with at least 3 years as a core faculty member in a CAPTE-accredited physical therapist education program as well as a substantial record of scholarly dissemination and external funding. They must demonstrate experience or professional development in educational administration, management, and leadership and familiarity with CAPTE accreditation requirements and/or experience with other accreditation and regulatory agencies. Ideal candidates will demonstrate excellent interpersonal and written communication skills, leadership skills, and a commitment to lifelong learning. A record of professional and institutional service and the ability to foster collaborative, innovative, and strategic work environments will strengthen the application. Knowledge, Skills & Abilities Application Process: Applicants must submit the following materials: Cover letter detailing their interest in the position and relevant experience including prior teaching experience and scholarship development. Curriculum Vitae (CV) highlighting academic and professional achievements. Names and contact information of three professional references Application Deadline: Application reviews are ongoing and will continue until the position is filled. At Springfield College we offer competitive and generous benefit options for employees and their families. Tuition benefits for employees and dependents Tuition exchange program for dependents Generous paid time off benefits Paid Sick Leave Benefits 403(b) retirement plan (TIAA) with a generous college match Medical, Dental and Vision plan(s) Health Reimbursement Arrangement (HRA) Flexible Spending Accounts (health care expenses and dependent care) Life and accident insurance plans Long-term disability Optional voluntary benefits Employee Assistance Program (EAP) Wellness benefits and programs Free use of our state-of-the-art Wellness Center Part-time employees earn pro-rated holiday, vacation and sick leave. To learn more about our Benefit Package, visit our Human Resources page. Employer Springfield College Address 263 Alden Street Springfield, Massachusetts, 01109 Phone 413-748-5678 Website http://www.springfield.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/springfieldcollege/jobs/5506506/associate-professor-or-professor-of-physical-therapy-and-dpt-program-director": {
      "title": "Associate Professor or Professor of Physical Therapy and DPT Program Director",
      "description": "Associate Professor or Professor of Physical Therapy and DPT Program Director Salary $120,000.00 - $148,000.00 Annually Location Springfield, MA Job Type Fiscal Faculty - 12 months Job Number 00449 Department Physical Therapy Opening Date 10/06/2026 Description Benefits Description The Springfield College Department of Physical Therapy invites applications for a full-time, twelve-month, tenure-track faculty position at the associate professor or professor level to serve as Department Chair and Doctor of Physical Therapy Program Director, beginning in July 2027. An earlier start date may be possible. This is an on-campus position in Springfield, Massachusetts. The Department offers an on-campus, 6.5-year early assurance pathway leading to a bachelor’s degree in Health Science/Pre-Physical Therapy and a Doctor of Physical Therapy degree. Grounded in lifelong learning and Springfield College’s Humanics Philosophy of educating the whole person in spirit, mind, and body for leadership in service to others, our faculty foster the knowledge, values, and skills for exemplary person-centered, evidence-informed practice. The Chair and Program Director will lead an established program recognized for strong student outcomes, nationally engaged faculty, clinical partnerships, and a dedicated alumni network. Through partnerships with consumers of physical therapy services, clinical sites, community organizations, and interprofessional colleagues, the Department contributes to the health and well-being of the greater Springfield community and beyond. The School of Health Sciences also includes Communication Sciences and Disorders, Health Science, Occupational Therapy, Physician Assistant Studies, Public Health, and Social Work. Its new 86,000-square-foot Brown Cooper Health Sciences Center includes interprofessional simulation facilities, a maker space, an activities of daily living suite, a human anatomy laboratory, and technology-supported classrooms. Collaboration, engaged learning, and global, intergenerational, and multicultural perspectives are priorities of the School. Founded in 1885, Springfield College is a private, coeducational institution whose undergraduate and graduate programs reflect its distinctive Humanics Philosophy. For more information, visit springfieldcollege.edu . Actual salaries will vary depending on multiple factors, including but not limited to degrees attained, experience, and other considerations permitted by law. Comprehensive compensation details, including any additional benefits, will be communicated upon finalization of the employment offer. Responsibilities The Chair and Program Director reports to the Dean of the School of Health Sciences and serves as the academic and administrative leader of the Department, with primary responsibility for the quality, effectiveness, accreditation, and development of the on-campus DPT program. Responsibilities include: • Lead departmental planning and set academic, administrative, and strategic priorities in collaboration with faculty, staff, the Dean, and College leadership. • Direct ongoing compliance with Commission on Accreditation in Physical Therapy Education (CAPTE) standards and required elements; coordinate reports, documentation, substantive-change notifications, reviews and site visits; and respond promptly to findings or required actions. • Maintain systematic assessment and continuous improvement of the curriculum, student learning, clinical education, graduation and licensure outcomes, faculty qualifications and sufficiency, and program resources. • Ensure that the DPT curriculum remains coherent, evidence-informed, and responsive to changes in physical therapy education, clinical practice, accreditation expectations, and health care. • Recruit, onboard, mentor, and evaluate faculty and staff; plan workload and staffing; support promotion and tenure processes; and foster a collegial culture of transparent communication and shared responsibility. • Advance departmental scholarship by mentoring faculty, fostering collaborative research and scholarly work, and expanding faculty and student opportunities to participate in research. • Lead student recruitment, progression, retention, and success in partnership with faculty and College offices; monitor outcomes and address academic concerns. • Oversee clinical education and strengthen relationships with clinical partners to support sufficient, appropriate, high-quality placements. • Develop and manage the departmental budget, staffing, instructional and laboratory needs, equipment, and facilities necessary for program quality and accreditation. • Work with the Dean and School leadership on enrollment, staffing, program development, and institutional priorities; advance interprofessional education across the School. • Represent the Department with alumni, clinical and community partners, professional organizations, prospective students and families, and other constituencies. • Maintain teaching, advising, scholarship, and institutional and professional service appropriate to a twelve-month faculty administrator. The Chair appointment includes administrative reassigned time and additional compensation in recognition of departmental leadership responsibilities. Actual compensation depends on qualifications, experience, and other lawful considerations; full details will be communicated during the offer process. Qualifications Required Qualifications • An earned academic doctoral degree, such as a PhD or EdD, in addition to the professional preparation required for physical therapist licensure. A DPT alone does not satisfy this requirement. • Current physical therapist licensure or eligibility for licensure in Massachusetts. • Academic credentials, teaching experience, and a scholarly record appropriate for appointment as associate professor or professor. • At least six years of full-time equivalent experience in higher education, including three years as a core faculty member in a CAPTE-accredited physical therapist education program. • A record of scholarly dissemination and continued engagement in scholarship. • Knowledge of CAPTE standards and processes, with experience contributing to accreditation reporting, program assessment, self-study, or compliance activities. • Experience or professional development in academic administration, management, and leadership; effective communication, collaboration, and decision-making; and commitment to student success and Springfield College’s mission. Preferred Qualifications • Experience as a chair, program director, associate program director, or in another significant academic leadership role. • Substantial CAPTE accreditation experience, including reports, self-study, compliance documentation, reviews, or site visits. • Experience with curriculum development, program assessment, and continuous improvement. • Experience mentoring faculty scholarship and supporting research and scholarly activity. • Experience with budgets, staffing and workload, enrollment planning, and academic resources. • Interprofessional collaboration and a record of professional and institutional service. Knowledge, Skills & Abilities Application Process: Applicants should submit a cover letter addressing academic leadership, physical therapy education, accreditation, teaching, and scholarship; a curriculum vitae; and names and contact information for three professional references. Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. At Springfield College we offer competitive and generous benefit options for employees and their families. Tuition benefits for employees and dependents Tuition exchange program for dependents Generous paid time off benefits Paid Sick Leave Benefits 403(b) retirement plan (TIAA) with a generous college match Medical, Dental and Vision plan(s) Health Reimbursement Arrangement (HRA) Flexible Spending Accounts (health care expenses and dependent care) Life and accident insurance plans Long-term disability Optional voluntary benefits Employee Assistance Program (EAP) Wellness benefits and programs Free use of our state-of-the-art Wellness Center Part-time employees earn pro-rated holiday, vacation and sick leave. To learn more about our Benefit Package, visit our Human Resources page. Employer Springfield College Address 263 Alden Street Springfield, Massachusetts, 01109 Phone 413-748-5678 Website http://www.springfield.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/springfieldcollege/jobs/5524483/adjunct-for-the-department-of-biology": {
      "title": "Adjunct for the Department of Biology",
      "description": "Adjunct for the Department of Biology Salary See Position Description Location Springfield, MA Job Type Adjunct Job Number 00451 Department Biology Chemistry Opening Date 10/21/2026 Description Benefits Description Springfield College invites applications for an adjunct with expertise in Microbiology to support the Biology/Chemistry Department housed in the School of Arts and Sciences. The Biology/Chemistry Department is an interdisciplinary department that includes the Biology and Environmental Studies majors and teaches both foundational science courses and a broad selection of upper-level science courses to the variety of a variety of programs across campus including numerous health science-related programs. Compensation for adjunct faculty with fewer than 3 years college teaching experience is $2820 for a 3-credit course. Compensation for adjunct faculty with 3 or more years college teaching experience is $3513 for a 3-credit course. Responsibilities We are seeking an instructor to teach two upper-level undergraduate lecture-based courses: Medical Microbiology and Immunology. These courses are scheduled for the Spring Semester Tu/Th 9:25-10:40 and Tu/Th 10:50-12:05, respectively. The successful candidate will teach both courses, including preparing and delivering course content, developing and evaluating student assessments, holding student support hours, and fostering an inclusive and engaging learning environment. Qualifications At minimum, applicants must have a master’s degree in a Biology or Microbiology-related field to qualify for the position. Must reside in one of the following states: MA, CT, WI, DE, TX, VT, FL Review of applications will begin immediately and will continue until the position is filled. Please submit a letter of interest that details your qualifications to teach these courses, your resume/CV, and the names and contact information for 3 professional references. This position is not eligible for benefits. Employer Springfield College Address 263 Alden Street Springfield, Massachusetts, 01109 Phone 413-748-5678 Website http://www.springfield.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/springfieldcollege/jobs/5535590/adjunct-for-the-physician-assistant-program": {
      "title": "Adjunct for the Physician Assistant Program",
      "description": "Adjunct for the Physician Assistant Program Salary See Position Description Location Springfield, MA Job Type Adjunct Job Number 00453 Department Physician Assistant Opening Date 10/30/2026 Description Benefits Description Springfield College is seeking dynamic and experienced adjunct faculty members to join our Physician Assistant program. As an adjunct faculty member, you will play a crucial role in delivering high-quality education to our PA students. Springfield College has a rich history of developing leaders in health and human services. Our DMSc program is designed for medical professionals seeking to advance their knowledge and expertise in medical science and leadership. Compensation for adjunct faculty with fewer than 3 years college teaching experience is $2820 for a 3-credit course Compensation for adjunct faculty with 3 or more years college teaching experience is $3513 for a 3-credit course. Responsibilities Responsibilities will depend on the role but could include: - Assisting in the PA program cadaver labs : Must be available for labs on Fridays 9am-3:30 pm during the semester - Provide timely and constructive feedback to students. - Engage in active communication with students to support their learning and professional growth. Qualifications Required Qualifications: - A minimum of 1 year of experience as a Physician Assistant or experience matching course expertise (e.g., pathologist, pharmacist, anatomist, etc.) - Strong commitment to student success and academic excellence. - Excellent communication and interpersonal skills. - Ability to work independently and as part of a team. Preferred Qualifications: - Previous experience teaching in a PA program or precepting clinical students. - Knowledge and experience with Learning Management Systems, particularly Brightspace. Application Process: Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis until the positions are filled. Interested candidates should submit the following materials: - Cover letter detailing their interest in the position and relevant experience including prior online teaching experience and scholarship development. - Curriculum Vitae (CV) highlighting academic and professional achievements. This position is not eligible for benefits. Employer Springfield College Address 263 Alden Street Springfield, Massachusetts, 01109 Phone 413-748-5678 Website http://www.springfield.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/springfieldcollege/jobs/5128466/adjunct-faculty-for-counseling-department": {
      "title": "Adjunct Faculty for Counseling Department",
      "description": "Adjunct Faculty for Counseling Department Salary See Position Description Location Springfield, MA Job Type Adjunct Job Number 00287 Department Counseling Department Opening Date 11/05/2026 Description Benefits Description The Counseling Department at Springfield College is seeking an adjunct faculty to teach undergraduate or graduate course work. The Counseling department is dedicated to preparing professional counselors in school, clinical mental Health and rehabilitation counseling. Founded in 1885, Springfield College is a private, coeducational institution offering undergraduate and graduate programs that reflect its distinctive Humanics Philosophy – the education of the whole person in spirit, mind, and body for leadership in service to humanity. For more information, please visit the College’s homepage. Compensation for adjunct faculty with fewer than 3 years college teaching experience is $2820 for a 3-credit course Compensation for adjunct faculty with 3 or more years college teaching experience is $3513 for a 3-credit course. Responsibilities The primary responsibilities of the position include teaching a graduate or undergraduate course, live or on line in the counseling department. It may include content courses such as human development, psychopharmacology, counseling theories, and clinical coursework such as practicum or internship. Qualifications Required Qualifications: Candidates must have a Master’s Degree in Counseling, Psychology or related field (Mental Health, Rehabilitation Counseling or School Counseling). Must reside in one of the following states: MA, CT, WI, DE, TX, VT, FL Preferred Qualifications Candidate should be licensed, certified or eligible with at least 3 years’ experience. We will prioritize candidates with current licensure in Massachusetts a well-articulated counselor identity; Experience teaching and providing clinical supervision to counselors-in-training; Ability to work independently and as part of a cohesive team. This position is not eligible for benefits. Employer Springfield College Address 263 Alden Street Springfield, Massachusetts, 01109 Phone 413-748-5678 Website http://www.springfield.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/springfieldcollege/jobs/5545007/adjunct-for-the-criminal-justice-department": {
      "title": "Adjunct for the Criminal Justice Department",
      "description": "Adjunct for the Criminal Justice Department Salary See Position Description Location Springfield, MA Job Type Adjunct Job Number 00459 Department Criminal Justice Opening Date 11/09/2026 Description Benefits Description Springfield College invites applications for a part-time adjunct faculty position for the Spring 2027 semester in Criminal Justice, teaching one section of CRIM 270: Constitutional Law. The ideal candidate will have demonstrated success in the classroom by developing and using effective teaching strategies that meet students' diverse needs and engage students in critical thinking and problem-solving. Compensation for adjunct faculty with fewer than 3 years college teaching experience is $2820 for a 3-credit course Compensation for adjunct faculty with 3 or more years college teaching experience is $3513 for a 3-credit course Responsibilities CRIM 270 Constitutional Law The course meets Wednesdays and Fridays, 12-1:15, in person. This course examines the historical concept of federalism. Students compare federal and state judiciary and review the concept of stare decisis. Most of the constitutional guarantees afforded suspects will be reviewed, including but not limited to, the right to confront witnesses, the privilege against self-incrimination, the right to be free from illegal search and seizure, double jeopardy protection and the right to a speedy public trial. Qualifications BA/BS required for all courses; MS, MA, or MEd required for graduate courses with PhD preferred. Please submit a CV/Resume, cover letter which indicates your interest and relevant expertise/experience for course/courses, and a list of 3 references. Knowledge, Skills & Abilities This position has been designed as forward facing and will meet face-to-face on the Springfield, MA campus. Please email chair of the Department of Humanities, Social Sciences, and Criminal Justice, Kate Dugan, with questions: kdugan@springfieldcollege.edu . Note that all applicants must complete this online application. This position is not eligible for benefits. Employer Springfield College Address 263 Alden Street Springfield, Massachusetts, 01109 Phone 413-748-5678 Website http://www.springfield.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/springfieldcollege/jobs/5545746/assistant-professor-of-athletic-leadership": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Athletic Leadership",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Athletic Leadership Salary $58,000.00 - $65,000.00 Annually Location Springfield, MA Job Type Academic Faculty - 9 months Job Number 00456 Department Physical Educ and Health Education Opening Date 11/10/2026 Description Benefits Description Founded in 1885, Springfield College is a private, coeducational institution guided by its distinctive Humanics Philosophy—the education of the whole person in spirit, mind, and body for leadership in service to humanity. In the School of Physical Education, Performance, and Sport Leadership, we steward his mission by engaging students in building the knowledge and skills to navigate their futures, challenging and supporting growth, fostering meaningful relationships, and cultivating leaders. Springfield College seeks applications for an Assistant Professor of Athletic Leadership to serve within the Department of Physical Education and Health Education. This full-time, nine-month, tenure-track appointment begins in September 2027. The candidate will primarily support the thriving Athletic Leadership master’s program, which prepares students to become inclusive, equity-minded leaders who create empowering sport experiences and positively impact the sport community. The program takes a holistic approach to leadership development, grounding evidence-based practice in theory, research, and application through active classroom engagement, field experience, and connections with experienced professionals. Actual salaries will vary depending on multiple factors, including but not limited to degrees attained, experience, and other considerations permitted by law. Comprehensive compensation details, including any additional benefits, will be communicated upon finalization of the employment offer. Responsibilities Responsibilities include advising graduate students, coordinating and/or supervising fieldwork, contributing to master’s and doctoral research supervision as appropriate, maintaining an independent or collaborative scholarly agenda, and providing service within and beyond the College. Expertise in athletic leadership and demonstrated inclusive excellence in teaching are expected. Qualifications Candidates must hold an earned doctorate from a regionally accredited institution in Athletic Administration, Coaching Education, Physical Education, or a related discipline. The ideal candidate will be able to teach graduate-level Athletic Administration and/or Coaching Education courses. At Springfield College we offer competitive and generous benefit options for employees and their families. Tuition benefits for employees and dependents Tuition exchange program for dependents Generous paid time off benefits Paid Sick Leave Benefits 403(b) retirement plan (TIAA) with a generous college match Medical, Dental and Vision plan(s) Health Reimbursement Arrangement (HRA) Flexible Spending Accounts (health care expenses and dependent care) Life and accident insurance plans Long-term disability Optional voluntary benefits Employee Assistance Program (EAP) Wellness benefits and programs Free use of our state-of-the-art Wellness Center Part-time employees earn pro-rated holiday, vacation and sick leave. To learn more about our Benefit Package, visit our Human Resources page. Employer Springfield College Address 263 Alden Street Springfield, Massachusetts, 01109 Phone 413-748-5678 Website http://www.springfield.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/springfieldcollege/jobs/5545785/assistant-professor-of-nutrition": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Nutrition",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Nutrition Salary $58,000.00 - $65,000.00 Annually Location Springfield, MA Job Type Academic Faculty - 9 months Job Number 00458 Department Exercise Science Opening Date 11/10/2026 Description Benefits Description Springfield College seeks applications for an Assistant Professor of Exercise Science within the Department of Exercise Science. This is a full-time, nine-month, program-track position within the School of Physical Education, Performance, and Sport Leadership beginning August 2027. Expertise in the field, demonstrated excellence in teaching, and a strong commitment to professional endeavors and service activities are expected. We are seeking a faculty member with expertise in Nutrition to teach a variety of undergraduate and graduate courses within the Nutrition Minor and various other undergraduate and graduate programs. Courses will likely include introduction to nutrition, lifespan nutrition, sport nutrition, advanced metabolism, and community nutrition. Additional responsibilities may include serving on thesis committees and academic advising. Founded in 1885, Springfield College is a private, coeducational institution offering undergraduate and graduate programs that reflect its distinctive Humanics Philosophy – the education of the whole person in spirit, mind, and body for leadership in service to humanity. In the School of Physical Education, Performance, and Sports Leadership, we steward this mission by engaging students in building knowledge and skills to navigate their future by challenging and supporting growth, fostering meaningful relationships, and cultivating leaders. We encourage applications from faculty candidates interested in helping us achieve this mission. Actual salaries will vary depending on multiple factors, including but not limited to degrees attained, experience, and other considerations permitted by law. Comprehensive compensation details, including any additional benefits, will be communicated upon finalization of the employment offer. Qualifications Required Qualifications: - Minimum of an earned master’s degree from a regionally accredited institution in nutrition, exercise science, or a closely related discipline. - Ability to deliver a wide variety of nutrition-based coursework. - Prior experience teaching in higher education. Preferred Qualifications: - Earned doctorate degree in Nutrition, Exercise Science, or related discipline from a regionally accredited institution (ABD considered depending on expected completion date) - Registered Dietitian - Certified Sports Nutritionist from the International Society of Sports Nutrition (C-ISSN) or other nutrition certification. - Experience in applied nutrition consultation. At Springfield College we offer competitive and generous benefit options for employees and their families. Tuition benefits for employees and dependents Tuition exchange program for dependents Generous paid time off benefits Paid Sick Leave Benefits 403(b) retirement plan (TIAA) with a generous college match Medical, Dental and Vision plan(s) Health Reimbursement Arrangement (HRA) Flexible Spending Accounts (health care expenses and dependent care) Life and accident insurance plans Long-term disability Optional voluntary benefits Employee Assistance Program (EAP) Wellness benefits and programs Free use of our state-of-the-art Wellness Center Part-time employees earn pro-rated holiday, vacation and sick leave. To learn more about our Benefit Package, visit our Human Resources page. Employer Springfield College Address 263 Alden Street Springfield, Massachusetts, 01109 Phone 413-748-5678 Website http://www.springfield.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/springfieldcollege/jobs/5545769/assistant-professor-of-physical-education-pedagogy": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Physical Education Pedagogy",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Physical Education Pedagogy Salary $58,000.00 - $65,000.00 Annually Location Springfield, MA Job Type Academic Faculty - 9 months Job Number 00457 Department Physical Educ and Health Education Opening Date 11/10/2026 Description Benefits Description Founded in 1885, Springfield College is a private, coeducational institution guided by its distinctive Humanics Philosophy—the education of the whole person in spirit, mind, and body for leadership in service to humanity. In the School of Physical Education, Performance, and Sport Leadership, we steward this mission by engaging students in building the knowledge and skills to navigate their futures, challenging and supporting growth, fostering meaningful relationships, and cultivating leaders. Springfield College seeks applications for an Assistant Professor of Physical Education to serve within the Department of Physical Education and Health Education. The successful candidate will join a nationally and internationally recognized Physical Education program with a rich tradition of preparing educators and a longstanding commitment to early, frequent, sequential, and developmental school-based field experiences. The position offers the opportunity to shape the next generation of physical educators through innovative teaching, empowering advising, independent or collaborative scholarship, and engagement with school and community partners. For more information, please visit www.springfieldcollege.edu . Actual salaries will vary depending on multiple factors, including but not limited to degrees attained, experience, and other considerations permitted by law. Comprehensive compensation details, including any additional benefits, will be communicated upon finalization of the employment offer. Responsibilities The selected candidate will primarily teach in the undergraduate Physical Education program, with opportunities to teach and mentor students in the master’s and doctoral programs. Faculty transport students to local school placements using a College-provided van. Responsibilities also include advising students, supervising fieldwork, developing an active scholarly agenda, and contributing service within and beyond the College. Qualifications - Candidates must hold an earned doctorate from a regionally accredited institution in Physical Education Teacher Education or a related discipline. - The ideal candidate will be prepared to teach undergraduate courses including Secondary Physical Education Methods, Assessment in Movement and Physical Education, and Motor Learning. - Because school-based field experiences are embedded in program coursework, the successful candidate must hold a valid driver’s license and meet the College’s driver eligibility and safety certification requirements. - Expertise in secondary physical education and assessment, along with inclusive, learner-centered instructional practices, is expected. At Springfield College we offer competitive and generous benefit options for employees and their families. Tuition benefits for employees and dependents Tuition exchange program for dependents Generous paid time off benefits Paid Sick Leave Benefits 403(b) retirement plan (TIAA) with a generous college match Medical, Dental and Vision plan(s) Health Reimbursement Arrangement (HRA) Flexible Spending Accounts (health care expenses and dependent care) Life and accident insurance plans Long-term disability Optional voluntary benefits Employee Assistance Program (EAP) Wellness benefits and programs Free use of our state-of-the-art Wellness Center Part-time employees earn pro-rated holiday, vacation and sick leave. To learn more about our Benefit Package, visit our Human Resources page. Employer Springfield College Address 263 Alden Street Springfield, Massachusetts, 01109 Phone 413-748-5678 Website http://www.springfield.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/springfieldcollege/jobs/5547435/assistant-professor-of-counseling": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Counseling",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Counseling Salary $65,000.00 - $70,000.00 Annually Location Springfield, MA Job Type Academic Faculty - 9 months Job Number 00465 Department Counseling Department Opening Date 11/11/2026 Description Benefits Description The Department of Counseling at Springfield College invites applications for a nine-month, tenure-track Assistant Professor of Counseling with expertise in Clinical Mental Health Counseling (CMHC). We seek a collaborative counselor educator committed to excellence in teaching, clinical preparation, scholarship, and preparing culturally responsive practitioners. The Department of Counseling offers graduate majors in School Counseling, Rehabilitation Counseling, and Clinical Mental Health Counseling, with concentrations in Art Therapy and Athletic Counseling within the Clinical Mental Health Counseling major. Grounded in Springfield College’s Humanics philosophy, the department prepares culturally responsive counselors through rigorous coursework, supervised clinical experiences, and a commitment to social justice and service. About Springfield College Founded in 1885, Springfield College is a private, coeducational institution offering undergraduate and graduate programs that reflect its distinctive Humanics philosophy—the education of the whole person in spirit, mind, and body for leadership in service to humanity. The Counseling Department seeks colleagues whose teaching, scholarship, and service advance this mission. Recognized worldwide as the birthplace of basketball, Springfield College welcomes candidates from all backgrounds and lived experiences who will contribute to the campus community. For more information, visit springfield.edu . Actual salaries will vary depending on multiple factors, including but not limited to degrees attained, experience, and other considerations permitted by law. Comprehensive compensation details, including any additional benefits, will be communicated upon finalization of the employment offer. Responsibilities The successful candidate will teach graduate courses in clinical mental health counseling and the core counseling curriculum. The position includes teaching responsibilities equivalent to a 12-credit load per semester. Courses may be offered face-to-face and online. Responsibilities include: Teaching courses aligned with the candidate’s expertise, potentially including Psychopathology: Diagnosis and Treatment Planning; Counseling Theories; Counseling Skills; Counseling Diverse Populations; Research and Program Evaluation; Professional Orientation and Ethical Practice; Clinical Seminar; and practicum and internship supervision. Supporting Spanish-language coursework and bilingual counseling preparation, based on the candidate’s language proficiency and expertise. Advising and mentoring graduate students to support academic progress, clinical development, and professional identity. Providing clinical instruction and fieldwork supervision in collaboration with the department’s clinical coordinator. Engaging in scholarship and maintaining an active or developing research agenda. Participating in recruitment, professional service, and collaborative departmental and College initiatives. Promoting an inclusive learning environment and preparing counselors to serve diverse individuals and communities. Qualifications Required Qualifications An earned doctorate in Counselor Education and Supervision or a closely related field. Licensure or ability to obtain licensure as a mental health counselor in Massachusetts within one year of hire. At least three years of relevant clinical experience. Demonstrated potential for effective graduate teaching and clinical supervision. Demonstrated commitment to culturally responsive practice, ethical professional preparation, and student success. Strong communication, organizational, and collaboration skills. Preferred Qualifications Doctorate from a CACREP-accredited Counselor Education and Supervision program. Experience teaching graduate counseling courses in face-to-face and online formats. Experience supervising counseling students and supporting clinical placements. Experience with CACREP accreditation, program assessment, or curriculum development. Evidence of scholarship or a developing research agenda relevant to clinical mental health counseling or counselor education. Spanish language proficiency, particularly the ability to support the department’s Spanish-language coursework, teach core counseling courses in Spanish, contribute to bilingual counseling preparation, and work with Spanish-speaking communities. Application Information Applicants should submit a letter of interest addressing their qualifications for the position; a curriculum vitae; a statement of teaching philosophy and research interests; and the names and contact information of three to five professional references. Applications will be reviewed starting January 1, 2027 and continue until the position is filled. Knowledge, Skills & Abilities We seek applicants whose teaching, research, outreach, and service will contribute to the department’s social justice and diversity focus while complementing existing departmental strengths. At Springfield College we offer competitive and generous benefit options for employees and their families. Tuition benefits for employees and dependents Tuition exchange program for dependents Generous paid time off benefits Paid Sick Leave Benefits 403(b) retirement plan (TIAA) with a generous college match Medical, Dental and Vision plan(s) Health Reimbursement Arrangement (HRA) Flexible Spending Accounts (health care expenses and dependent care) Life and accident insurance plans Long-term disability Optional voluntary benefits Employee Assistance Program (EAP) Wellness benefits and programs Free use of our state-of-the-art Wellness Center Part-time employees earn pro-rated holiday, vacation and sick leave. To learn more about our Benefit Package, visit our Human Resources page. Employer Springfield College Address 263 Alden Street Springfield, Massachusetts, 01109 Phone 413-748-5678 Website http://www.springfield.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/springfieldcollege/jobs/5547432/assistant-professor-of-counseling-and-art-therapy-program-director": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Counseling and Art Therapy Program Director",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Counseling and Art Therapy Program Director Salary $65,000.00 - $70,000.00 Annually Location Springfield, MA Job Type Academic Faculty - 9 months Job Number 00464 Department Counseling Department Opening Date 11/11/2026 Description Benefits Description The Department of Counseling at Springfield College invites applications for a nine-month, tenure-track Assistant Professor of Counseling to oversee the Art Therapy concentration within the Clinical Mental Health Counseling (CMHC) major. We seek a collaborative counselor educator and credentialed art therapist committed to teaching, clinical preparation, and preparing culturally responsive practitioners. About Springfield College Founded in 1885, Springfield College is a private, coeducational institution offering undergraduate and graduate programs that reflect its distinctive Humanics philosophy—the education of the whole person in spirit, mind, and body for leadership in service to humanity. The Counseling Department seeks colleagues whose teaching, scholarship, and service advance this mission. For more information, visit springfield.edu . Actual salaries will vary depending on multiple factors, including but not limited to degrees attained, experience, and other considerations permitted by law. Comprehensive compensation details, including any additional benefits, will be communicated upon finalization of the employment offer. Responsibilities The successful candidate will provide leadership for the Art Therapy concentration and teach graduate courses in art therapy and clinical mental health counseling. This position offers opportunities for leadership, creativity, and collaboration in helping to shape our newly designed Art Therapy concentration within the Clinical Mental Health Counseling program in the Department of Counseling, expanding students’ pathways to licensure and employment while preserving fine art focus and preparation for professional art therapy credentials. The position includes teaching and administrative responsibilities equivalent to a 12 credit load per semester, with assigned administrative responsibilities recognized within that load. Courses may be offered face-to-face and online. Responsibilities include: Directing the Art Therapy concentration, including administration, organization, curriculum development, course scheduling, recruitment, and program assessment. Consult on the recruitment and hire of qualified faculty to support teaching within the art therapy concentration, and regularly convene an advisory committee for the purpose of maintaining accreditation and guiding curricular development Leading our re-accreditation and annual review of CAAHEP/ACATE, including documentation, reporting, continuous quality review and improvement, and coordination with Counseling Department leadership. Providing academic oversight and supporting the integration of art therapy preparation with the CMHC curriculum and applicable accreditation and professional credentialing requirements. Ensuring that all Art Therapy students have faculty advisors and receive support for academic progress, professional development, and credentialing preparation. Teaching courses aligned with the candidate’s expertise, potentially including art therapy foundations and theory, assessment, human development and specialized populations, research, advanced seminar, and core counseling courses. Coordinating art therapy fieldwork with the department’s clinical coordinator, including identifying appropriate placements, supporting site supervisors, and monitoring students’ clinical development. Providing clinical instruction and supervision consistent with the candidate’s qualifications and applicable program requirements. Engaging in scholarship, professional service, and collaborative departmental and College initiatives. Promoting an inclusive learning environment that supports students’ professional identities as counselors and art therapists. Qualifications Required Qualifications A doctorate in Counseling or related field A master’s degree in Art Therapy or a closely related degree that meets professional art therapy credentialing requirements. Current Board Certified Registered Art Therapist (ATR-BC) certification through the Art Therapy Credentials Board, or an equivalent national art therapy certification meeting CAAHEP/ACATE requirements through a certification organization accredited by NCCA, ANSI, or under ISO. Licensure or ability to obtain licensure as a mental health counselor in Massachusetts within one year of hire. At least three years of relevant clinical experience. Demonstrated commitment to culturally responsive practice, ethical professional preparation, and student success. Strong organizational, communication, and collaborative leadership skills. Preferred Qualifications An earned doctorate in Counselor Education and Supervision, ideally from a CACREP-accredited Counselor Education and Supervision program, is highly preferred. Experience teaching graduate courses in art therapy and counseling. Experience with CAAHEP/ACATE accreditation, program assessment, curriculum development, or program administration. Experience supervising art therapy students and coordinating clinical placements. Evidence of scholarship or a developing research agenda relevant to art therapy, counseling, or counselor education. Application Information Applicants should submit a letter of interest addressing their qualifications for teaching and Art Therapy program leadership; a curriculum vitae; a statement of teaching philosophy and research interests; and the names and contact information of three to five professional references. Applications will be reviewed starting January 1, 2027 and continue until the position is filled. At Springfield College we offer competitive and generous benefit options for employees and their families. Tuition benefits for employees and dependents Tuition exchange program for dependents Generous paid time off benefits Paid Sick Leave Benefits 403(b) retirement plan (TIAA) with a generous college match Medical, Dental and Vision plan(s) Health Reimbursement Arrangement (HRA) Flexible Spending Accounts (health care expenses and dependent care) Life and accident insurance plans Long-term disability Optional voluntary benefits Employee Assistance Program (EAP) Wellness benefits and programs Free use of our state-of-the-art Wellness Center Part-time employees earn pro-rated holiday, vacation and sick leave. To learn more about our Benefit Package, visit our Human Resources page. Employer Springfield College Address 263 Alden Street Springfield, Massachusetts, 01109 Phone 413-748-5678 Website http://www.springfield.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/springfieldcollege/jobs/5547425/assistant-professor-of-criminology-and-social-science": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Criminology and Social Science",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Criminology and Social Science Salary $62,000.00 - $67,000.00 Annually Location Springfield, MA Job Type Academic Faculty - 9 months Job Number 00463 Department Humanities and Social Sciences Opening Date 11/11/2026 Description Benefits Description The Springfield College Department of Humanities, Social Sciences, and Criminal Justice invites applications for a nine-month, tenure-track Assistant Professor of Criminology and Social Science to begin in AY 2027-28. The ideal candidate will be a contemporary social scientist whose work examines crime, law, inequality, and social institutions as interconnected features of social life. Candidates should demonstrate strength in theories of crime or social control, and contemporary social-science methods. The Department of Humanities, Social Sciences and Criminal Justice is a vibrant, multidisciplinary department that houses a thriving Criminal Justice major, a History major, and diverse minors such as Philosophy, Pre-Law, Public History and Museum Studies, and Social Justice. The ideal candidate will be able to contribute to some of these programs, depending on their expertise, be willing to collaborate with relevant programs across the College, and be able to advise students across relevant programs. The ideal candidate will also contribute to program evaluation and evidence-based curricular improvement. Founded in 1885, Springfield College is a private, coeducational institution offering undergraduate and graduate programs that reflect its distinctive Humanics Philosophy – the education of the whole person in spirit, mind, and body for leadership in service to humanity. Springfield College offers over 40 undergraduate and 14 graduate areas of study. It is world renowned as the birthplace of basketball. Springfield College is consistently ranked in the top tier in its category – Master’s-North Region - in the “America’s Best Colleges” issue of U.S. News & World Report. For more information, please visit the College’s homepage at www.springfieldcollege.edu . Actual salaries will vary depending on multiple factors, including but not limited to degrees attained, experience, and other considerations permitted by law. Comprehensive compensation details, including any additional benefits, will be communicated upon finalization of the employment offer. Responsibilities The candidate will contribute primarily to the Criminal Justice program and the interdisciplinary social science areas of the College’s Core Curriculum. Productive areas of specialization could include policing and communities, race and punishment, courts and corrections, restorative justice, violence and victimization, migration and social control, socio-legal studies, gender and justice, organizations and public policy, or comparative justice systems. Responsibilities include teaching 12 credits/semester, providing attentive and caring student advising and mentoring, ongoing scholarship in the field, and service to the department. Springfield College adheres to the Boyer Model of Scholarship which includes pedagogical research, community-engaged scholarship, and integrative and applied scholarship as scholarly activity. Qualifications Successful candidates should anticipate having earned a Ph.D. in Criminology, Criminal Justice, Sociology, Anthropology, Political Science, Public Policy, Socio-Legal Studies, or a closely related field, by May 2027. The College is seeking applicants with well-established research and strong potential for scholarly publication and achievement, as well as demonstrated inclusive excellence in teaching and advising students. Initial review of applications will begin by December 15, 2026 and will continue until the position is filled. To complete the application process, applicants will need to submit a letter of interest, curriculum vitae, teaching and research statements, and the names and contact information for 3-5 professional references. At Springfield College we offer competitive and generous benefit options for employees and their families. Tuition benefits for employees and dependents Tuition exchange program for dependents Generous paid time off benefits Paid Sick Leave Benefits 403(b) retirement plan (TIAA) with a generous college match Medical, Dental and Vision plan(s) Health Reimbursement Arrangement (HRA) Flexible Spending Accounts (health care expenses and dependent care) Life and accident insurance plans Long-term disability Optional voluntary benefits Employee Assistance Program (EAP) Wellness benefits and programs Free use of our state-of-the-art Wellness Center Part-time employees earn pro-rated holiday, vacation and sick leave. To learn more about our Benefit Package, visit our Human Resources page. Employer Springfield College Address 263 Alden Street Springfield, Massachusetts, 01109 Phone 413-748-5678 Website http://www.springfield.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/springfieldcollege/jobs/5547420/assistant-professor-of-psychology": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Psychology",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Psychology Salary $65,000.00 - $70,000.00 Annually Location Springfield, MA Job Type Academic Faculty - 9 months Job Number 00462 Department Psychology Opening Date 11/11/2026 Description Benefits Description Springfield College’s Department of Psychology invites applications for a 9-month, tenure-track position Assistant Professor of Psychology, with specialization in counseling or clinical psychology. The Psychology Department, housed in the School of Arts and Sciences, is a vibrant department that includes a large undergraduate major, and a Counseling Psychology Psy.D. program. This position will primarily support the APA-accredited Counseling Psychology PsyD program, though the candidate should expect to teach for the highly-enrolled undergraduate program as well. The expected teaching and supervision load is the equivalent of 12 credits each semester. Courses to be taught may include: Group Counseling; Ethical, Legal and Professional Issues in Counseling; Qualitative Research Methods; undergraduate Abnormal Psychology and Practicum and Doctoral Supervision, in addition to advising, mentoring, and departmental service. Founded in 1885, Springfield College is a private, coeducational institution offering undergraduate and graduate programs that reflect its distinctive Humanics Philosophy – the education of the whole person in spirit, mind, and body for leadership in service to humanity. Springfield College offers over 40 undergraduate and 14 graduate areas of study. It is world renowned as the birthplace of basketball. Springfield College is consistently ranked in the top tier in its category – Master’s-North Region - in the “America’s Best Colleges” issue of U.S. News & World Report. For more information, please visit the College’s homepage at www.springfieldcollege.edu. Actual salaries will vary depending on multiple factors, including but not limited to degrees attained, experience, and other considerations permitted by law. Comprehensive compensation details, including any additional benefits, will be communicated upon finalization of the employment offer. Qualifications Successful candidates should anticipate having earned a Ph.D. in Counseling Psychology or Clinical Psychology by May 2027. The College is seeking applicants with a record of research publication and scholarly achievement, and demonstrated inclusive excellence in teaching and advising students at the graduate level. Prospective applicants with experience mentoring graduate student research are encouraged to apply. Review of applications will begin after the priority deadline of December 15, 2026 and continue until filled. To complete the application process, applicants will need to submit a letter of interest, curriculum vitae, a statement of teaching and research interests, and the names and contact information for 3-5 professional references. In addition, please include evidence of teaching effectiveness; this may include: a summary of teaching evaluations, a teaching observation letter from a faculty supervisor, and/or a teaching portfolio (Upload as Additional Documents). At Springfield College we offer competitive and generous benefit options for employees and their families. Tuition benefits for employees and dependents Tuition exchange program for dependents Generous paid time off benefits Paid Sick Leave Benefits 403(b) retirement plan (TIAA) with a generous college match Medical, Dental and Vision plan(s) Health Reimbursement Arrangement (HRA) Flexible Spending Accounts (health care expenses and dependent care) Life and accident insurance plans Long-term disability Optional voluntary benefits Employee Assistance Program (EAP) Wellness benefits and programs Free use of our state-of-the-art Wellness Center Part-time employees earn pro-rated holiday, vacation and sick leave. To learn more about our Benefit Package, visit our Human Resources page. Employer Springfield College Address 263 Alden Street Springfield, Massachusetts, 01109 Phone 413-748-5678 Website http://www.springfield.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/springfieldcollege/jobs/5547411/assistant-professor-of-religion": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Religion",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Religion Salary $60,000.00 - $65,000.00 Annually Location Springfield, MA Job Type Academic Faculty - 9 months Job Number 00461 Department Humanities and Social Sciences Opening Date 11/11/2026 Description Benefits Description The Department of Humanities, Social Sciences, and Criminal Justice in the School of Arts and Sciences seeks to hire a nine-month, tenure-track position, Assistant Professor of Religion. Responsibilities include teaching 12 credits/semester, primarily in the Core Curriculum (General Education), ongoing scholarship in the field, and service to the department. Founded in 1885, Springfield College is a private, coeducational institution offering undergraduate and graduate programs that reflect its distinctive Humanics Philosophy – the education of the whole person in spirit, mind, and body for leadership in service to humanity. Springfield College offers over 40 undergraduate and 14 graduate areas of study. It is world renowned as the birthplace of basketball. Springfield College is consistently ranked in the top tier in its category – Master’s-North Region - in the “America’s Best Colleges” issue of U.S. News & World Report. For more information, please visit the College’s homepage at www.springfieldcollege.edu . Actual salaries will vary depending on multiple factors, including but not limited to degrees attained, experience, and other considerations permitted by law. Comprehensive compensation details, including any additional benefits, will be communicated upon finalization of the employment offer. Responsibilities The ability to engage students across the college and at all levels in the Spiritual & Ethical Perspectives domain of the Springfield College Core Curriculum is an essential qualification. Possible courses to be taught, dependent on the expertise of the candidate, could include any of the following: Health Care Ethics, Religions of the World, Liberation Theologies, Religion & Food, Religion in America, and The Good Life. This position will also regularly teach our interdisciplinary first-year seminar. The ideal candidate would be prepared to develop new courses such as Introduction to Bioethics or AI, Ethics, and Medicine. The ideal candidate will be willing to be a collaborative partner with disciplines across campus. The department is working to develop approaches to Core Curriculum coursework that draw connections between majors in the health sciences and physical education, such as through our new Health Humanities minor. and this position is an important part of that project. Qualifications A Master’s degree is required and a PhD preferred in any of the contributing disciplines of Philosophy, Theology, or Religious Studies. Candidates with interdisciplinary teaching experience and expertise are especially welcome. Experience integrating experiential education (for example, place-based or community-engaged learning) will be a strength for candidates applying to this position. Initial review of applications will begin by December 15, 2026 and will continue until the position is filled. To complete the application process, applicants will need to submit a letter of interest, curriculum vitae, teaching and research statements, and the names and contact information for 3-5 professional references. At Springfield College we offer competitive and generous benefit options for employees and their families. Tuition benefits for employees and dependents Tuition exchange program for dependents Generous paid time off benefits Paid Sick Leave Benefits 403(b) retirement plan (TIAA) with a generous college match Medical, Dental and Vision plan(s) Health Reimbursement Arrangement (HRA) Flexible Spending Accounts (health care expenses and dependent care) Life and accident insurance plans Long-term disability Optional voluntary benefits Employee Assistance Program (EAP) Wellness benefits and programs Free use of our state-of-the-art Wellness Center Part-time employees earn pro-rated holiday, vacation and sick leave. To learn more about our Benefit Package, visit our Human Resources page. Employer Springfield College Address 263 Alden Street Springfield, Massachusetts, 01109 Phone 413-748-5678 Website http://www.springfield.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/springfieldcollege/jobs/5547475/assistant-professor-of-journalism": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Journalism",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Journalism Salary $62,000.00 - $67,000.00 Annually Location Springfield, MA Job Type Academic Faculty - 9 months Job Number 00466 Department Literature Writing and Journalism Opening Date 11/11/2026 Description Benefits Description Springfield College invites applications for a nine-month, full-time, program-track Assistant Professor of Journalism with expertise in journalism writing, reporting, and editing across print and digital platforms. The successful candidate will teach the writing-intensive courses in the Sports & Multimedia Journalism major, with an emphasis on sports news writing, feature writing, sports reporting, multimedia storytelling, and interviewing. Candidates should demonstrate a strong commitment to developing students’ reporting, research, writing, and editing skills while preparing them for careers in contemporary sports journalism. Review of applications will begin after the priority deadline January 1st and continue until filled. Founded in 1885, Springfield College is a private, coeducational institution offering undergraduate and graduate programs that reflect its distinctive Humanics Philosophy – the education of the whole person in spirit, mind, and body for leadership in service to humanity. Springfield College offers over 40 undergraduate and 14 graduate areas of study. It is world renowned as the birthplace of basketball. Springfield College is consistently ranked in the top tier in its category – Master’s-North Region - in the “America’s Best Colleges” issue of U.S. News & World Report. For more information, please visit the College’s homepage at www.springfieldcollege.edu . Actual salaries will vary depending on multiple factors, including but not limited to degrees attained, experience, and other considerations permitted by law. Comprehensive compensation details, including any additional benefits, will be communicated upon finalization of the employment offer. Responsibilities The instructional responsibilities are the equivalent of 12-credits per semester, and will include didactic journalism courses, as well as overseeing and advising the campus newspaper and Pride Sports Journal. Additional expectations include ongoing scholarly or professional activity, the development and enhancement of journalism curriculum, and advising and service to our vibrant undergraduate major in Sports & Multimedia Journalism Qualifications The minimum requirements include a Master’s Degree in Journalism or a closely related field and significant professional experience in sports journalism. Applicants should include a cover letter, CV, a representative sample of journalistic work; as well as evidence of teaching effectiveness: include a summary of teaching evaluations, a teaching observation letter from a faculty supervisor, or a teaching portfolio (Upload as Additional Documents); and the contact information for three professional references. The start date for the position is August 2027. Review of applications will begin after the priority deadline January 1st and continue until filled. At Springfield College we offer competitive and generous benefit options for employees and their families. Tuition benefits for employees and dependents Tuition exchange program for dependents Generous paid time off benefits Paid Sick Leave Benefits 403(b) retirement plan (TIAA) with a generous college match Medical, Dental and Vision plan(s) Health Reimbursement Arrangement (HRA) Flexible Spending Accounts (health care expenses and dependent care) Life and accident insurance plans Long-term disability Optional voluntary benefits Employee Assistance Program (EAP) Wellness benefits and programs Free use of our state-of-the-art Wellness Center Part-time employees earn pro-rated holiday, vacation and sick leave. To learn more about our Benefit Package, visit our Human Resources page. Employer Springfield College Address 263 Alden Street Springfield, Massachusetts, 01109 Phone 413-748-5678 Website http://www.springfield.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/springfieldcollege/jobs/5547906/assistant-professor-of-microbiology": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Microbiology",
      "description": "Assistant Professor of Microbiology Salary $60,000.00 - $65,000.00 Annually Location Springfield, MA Job Type Academic Faculty - 9 months Job Number 00460 Department Biology Chemistry Opening Date 11/12/2026 Description Benefits Description The Department of Biology/Chemistry invites applications for a nine-month, tenure-track Assistant Professor of Microbiology beginning in the Fall 2027 semester. We seek an enthusiastic and innovative educator-scholar who is committed to excellence in undergraduate teaching, evidence-based pedagogical practices, and scholarly work that actively engages undergraduate students or addresses important pedagogical questions. The successful candidate will contribute to a collaborative, student-centered academic community through engaging instruction, scholarly activity, advising, and mentorship. We are particularly interested in candidates who integrate inclusive teaching practices and authentic research experiences into the undergraduate curriculum. Candidates with experience, or a strong interest in, developing or implementing Course-based Undergraduate Research Experiences (CUREs) are highly desirable. Springfield College also adheres to the Boyer Model of Scholarship which also includes pedagogical research as scholarly activity. The successful candidate will join a department that values the integration of teaching and scholarship and recognizes experiential learning as an important component of student learning. We seek a colleague who will contribute to a collaborative departmental culture, engage students as emerging scientists, and help prepare graduates for careers in the life sciences, health professions, graduate study, and related fields and in alignment with the College’s Humanics mission. Founded in 1885, Springfield College is a private, coeducational institution offering undergraduate and graduate programs that reflect its distinctive Humanics Philosophy – the education of the whole person in spirit, mind, and body for leadership in service to humanity. Springfield College offers over 40 undergraduate and 14 graduate areas of study. It is world renowned as the birthplace of basketball. Springfield College is consistently ranked in the top tier in its category – Master’s-North Region - in the “America’s Best Colleges” issue of U.S. News & World Report. For more information, please visit the College’s homepage at www.springfieldcollege.edu. Springfield College welcomes candidates from all backgrounds and lived experiences, who will contribute to the campus community. Actual salaries will vary depending on multiple factors, including but not limited to degrees attained, experience, and other considerations permitted by law. Comprehensive compensation details, including any additional benefits, will be communicated upon finalization of the employment offer. Responsibilities The successful candidate will be expected to: Teach undergraduate courses in microbiology and related areas of biology, with opportunities to contribute to introductory biology, upper-level electives, and laboratory-based courses according to departmental needs and the candidate's expertise. Incorporate authentic research experiences, inquiry-based learning, or other forms of experiential learning practices into undergraduate courses. Contribute to curriculum development and assessment initiatives that support student learning and program-level goals and learning outcomes. Foster an inclusive learning environment that supports students with diverse backgrounds, experiences, and academic goals. Integrate research into the curriculum through CUREs, research-based laboratory courses, or other approaches that provide students with authentic opportunities to contribute to scientific knowledge. Pursue scholarly dissemination through peer-reviewed publications, presentations at professional conferences, and other appropriate venues, with undergraduate students included as collaborators when warranted. Contribute to departmental and institutional service. Collaborate with colleagues across disciplines to develop interdisciplinary teaching and research opportunities. Participate in professional development activities that advance teaching effectiveness, scholarly productivity, and student success. Qualifications Required qualifications: A Ph.D. in Microbiology or a closely related discipline by the time of appointment, ABD considered. The ability to develop and teach engaging undergraduate lecture and laboratory courses. Preferred qualifications: Experience developing, implementing, or assessing Course-based Undergraduate Research Experiences (CUREs). Evidence of using evidence-based pedagogical practices, including active learning, inquiry-based instruction, or other approaches that promote student engagement and learning. Experience mentoring undergraduate researchers and supporting student participation in scholarly presentations or publications. A research agenda that offers accessible, meaningful projects for undergraduate students while supporting sustained scholarly productivity. Experience pursuing external research funding or developing competitive grant proposals. Experience integrating microbiological techniques, data analysis, and contemporary research methods into undergraduate laboratory instruction. Applicants should submit the following materials: Cover letter describing their interest in the position and alignment with the department's teaching and research priorities. Curriculum vitae documenting academic preparation, teaching experience, research accomplishments, and scholarly activity. Combined teaching and research statement describing their teaching philosophy, evidence-based instructional practices, commitment to inclusive teaching, and approach to undergraduate laboratory education. The statement should also describe their future plans for scholarly work appropriate for a primarily undergraduate institution and opportunities for undergraduate student participation including descriptions of CUREs or research experiences if applicable. Contact information for three professional references. Review of applications will begin after the priority deadline of December 11, 2026 and continue until filled with a start date of the Fall 2027 semester. At Springfield College we offer competitive and generous benefit options for employees and their families. Tuition benefits for employees and dependents Tuition exchange program for dependents Generous paid time off benefits Paid Sick Leave Benefits 403(b) retirement plan (TIAA) with a generous college match Medical, Dental and Vision plan(s) Health Reimbursement Arrangement (HRA) Flexible Spending Accounts (health care expenses and dependent care) Life and accident insurance plans Long-term disability Optional voluntary benefits Employee Assistance Program (EAP) Wellness benefits and programs Free use of our state-of-the-art Wellness Center Part-time employees earn pro-rated holiday, vacation and sick leave. To learn more about our Benefit Package, visit our Human Resources page. Employer Springfield College Address 263 Alden Street Springfield, Massachusetts, 01109 Phone 413-748-5678 Website http://www.springfield.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/springfieldcollege/jobs/5549072/adjunct-instructor-for-college-writing": {
      "title": "Adjunct Instructor for College Writing",
      "description": "Adjunct Instructor for College Writing Salary See Position Description Location Springfield, MA Job Type Adjunct Job Number 00468 Department Literature Writing and Journalism Opening Date 11/13/2026 Description Benefits Description Springfield College is accepting applications for part-time instructors in College Writing (our first-year writing sequence) for openings beginning in January. Compensation for adjunct faculty with fewer than 3 years college teaching experience is $2820 for a 3-credit course Compensation for adjunct faculty with 3 or more years college teaching experience is $3513 for a 3-credit course. Qualifications Requirements include a minimum of a Master’s Degree in English/Composition or a related field. Candidates must have excellent communication skills, and demonstrated creativity and flexibility in teaching students with varied preparation levels. College level teaching experience is preferred. Review of applications will begin immediately and will continue until the position is filled. This position is not eligible for benefits. Employer Springfield College Address 263 Alden Street Springfield, Massachusetts, 01109 Phone 413-748-5678 Website http://www.springfield.edu Apply"
    }
  },
